        {
          "vo": "It feels tailor-made for you. It describes everyone. That's the whole trick, vague statements general enough to fit almost any human alive.",
          "scene": "story",
          "emotion": "happy-talk",
          "room": "bedroom-night",
          "camera": {
            "zoomTo": [
              0.52,
              0.46,
              1.2
            ]
          },
          "mascots": [
            {
              "costume": "pajamas",
              "pose": "idle",
              "emotion": "happy-talk",
              "x": 660,
              "y": 520,
              "scale": 1.25,
              "talking": true,
              "accessory": "nightcap"
            }
          ],
          "props": [
            {
              "name": "horoscope-card",
              "x": 1300,
              "y": 500,
              "scale": 1.15,
              "appearAt": 4
            },
            {
              "name": "blanket",
              "x": 660,
              "y": 812,
              "scale": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "vo": "In nineteen forty-nine, psychologist Bertram Forer gave his students a personality test, then handed every single one the identical description, secretly copied from a newsstand astrology book.",
          "scene": "explainer",
          "prop": "horoscope-card",
          "emotion": "serious-expert"
        },
        {
          "vo": "He asked them to rate how accurate it was, just for them. On average, they rated it four point three out of five. The same words. For everyone.",
          "scene": "statCard",
          "value": 4.3,
          "suffix": "/5",
          "pictogram": "none",
          "emotion": "excited"
        },
        {
          "vo": "Personality quizzes, psychics, fortune cookies, all run on this. The more a description flatters you and stays vague, the more personal it feels. Being seen is lovely. Just make sure it's actually you being seen.",
